module runtime.abi_world

/// The process world lock - the port's GIL - as a PORTABLE contract.
///
/// WHY IT EXISTS. The port's runtime keeps process-wide mutable state that the
/// native build protects with a mix of Rust's own thread safety and a registry
/// mutex: the bump arena and the sweepability registry (`abi_raw`), the
/// temp-root and mark worklists (`abi_gcengine`), the many `var` singletons in
/// the ABI modules (interned strings, box tables, WSA scratch), and the
/// managed-heap mark that walks them. None of that is re-entrant. A second OS
/// thread running Pickle code concurrently would corrupt it.
///
/// The cheapest correct model for a runtime of this shape is a single world
/// lock: exactly one thread executes Pickle code, every other thread is parked
/// on the lock. It is the same discipline the JIT and the collector already
/// assume, so no allocator, descriptor, or collector walk has to learn a new
/// concurrency contract. The cost is parallelism, which this port does not
/// have today anyway.
///
/// THE CONTRACT, IN PLATFORM-NEUTRAL TERMS:
///
///   worldAcquire()      block until this thread owns the one world lock
///   worldRelease()      give it up; the caller must own it
///   worldHeld()         snapshot probe, meaningful only with no peer running
///   worldPark(cell)     release the lock and sleep on a condition cell;
///                       reacquire before returning (spurious wakes possible)
///   worldWake(cell)     wake one thread parked on the cell
///   worldWakeAll(cell)  wake every thread parked on the cell
///
///   - flat ownership: a thread never acquires twice without releasing
///   - blocking, not spinning: the OS parks the waiter
///   - released around blocking syscalls (recv/send/accept/connect, sleep,
///     join, yield, and the sync primitives) so the peer can run
///   - a condition cell is one zero-initialized word owned by the caller; its
///     encoding is a backend detail, and every wait is a predicate loop
///
/// THIS MODULE IS THE SEAM. Every portable runtime module imports these three
/// functions and never names an OS lock. The Win32 implementation lives in
/// `runtime/abi_world_win.pkl` (SRWLOCK, zero-initialized image data); a POSIX
/// build supplies `runtime/abi_world_posix.pkl` with the same `winWorld*`
/// entry points backed by `pthread_mutex_t`/futex and a different owner-word
/// layout. The build's platform filter selects exactly one backend file, so
/// the portable modules are unchanged. The same rule applies to every future
/// parking primitive: the OS call belongs in a `_win`/`_posix` module, the
/// wait/notify contract belongs here or in the module that owns the object.
///
/// WHAT MUST NOT HAPPEN. No `win_acquire_srw_exclusive`, `CRITICAL_SECTION`,
/// `pthread_mutex_*`, or futex call may appear outside a backend module. A
/// second hand-rolled spinlock is also forbidden: the shadow registry uses
/// `abi_world_win`'s lock-cell helper, and `abi_sync`'s waits will use the
/// same backend seam when they become real parking.

import { winWorldAcquire, winWorldRelease, winWorldHeld, winWorldPark, winWorldWake, winWorldWakeAll } from runtime.abi_world_win
import { abiGcEnterNative, abiGcLeaveNative } from runtime.abi_gcengine

/// Take the world lock, blocking until it is free.
///
/// A blocked acquirer is in a native section: it touches no managed object
/// while it waits, so the collector does not count it. `abiGcLeaveNative`
/// restores the count and runs a safepoint before the caller can use the heap
/// again. Both are no-ops until the process has a second thread.
fn worldAcquire() {
    abiGcEnterNative()
    winWorldAcquire()
    abiGcLeaveNative()
}

/// Release the world lock. The caller must own it.
///
/// The release starts a native section: every caller continues into a blocking
/// OS call (or a condition wait) that must not hold up a collection, and pairs
/// this with `worldAcquire` before it touches the heap again.
fn worldRelease() {
    abiGcEnterNative()
    winWorldRelease()
}

/// Non-blocking probe: does the lock look owned right now?
///
/// This is a snapshot, not ownership. The only caller is the startup net in
/// `pickle_thread_spawn`, which uses it to acquire the lock lazily on an image
/// whose entry trampoline did not run `pickle_runtime_init` (the JIT lane).
/// Nothing may use it as an ownership test inside a running runtime body.
fn worldHeld() -> bool {
    return winWorldHeld()
}

/// Park the calling thread on the condition cell `cell`, releasing the world
/// lock while asleep and reacquiring it before returning.
///
/// `cell` is the address of one zero-initialized word owned by the caller's
/// primitive (a sync state block today). Its representation is a backend
/// detail. Parking can wake spuriously, so the caller must wrap the call in a
/// predicate loop:
///
///     while (!ready()) {
///         worldPark(cell)
///     }
///
/// The caller must own the world lock. The release/park is atomic with respect
/// to the waker, which also holds the lock - that is what rules out a lost
/// wakeup between the predicate check and the sleep.
///
/// The sleep is a native section: the world lock is released, so a collection
/// on another thread must not wait for this one. `abiGcLeaveNative` runs the
/// safepoint after the lock is reacquired.
fn worldPark(cell: int) {
    abiGcEnterNative()
    winWorldPark(cell)
    abiGcLeaveNative()
}

/// Wake one thread parked on `cell`; a no-op when nobody is waiting.
fn worldWake(cell: int) {
    winWorldWake(cell)
}

/// Wake every thread parked on `cell`.
fn worldWakeAll(cell: int) {
    winWorldWakeAll(cell)
}
